                <pb facs="1022" n="974" sameAs="frus:frus1964-68v24/pg974"/>

                <head>580. Memorandum From William H.
                        Brubeck of the National Security Council Staff to the President’s
                    Special Assistant for National Security Affairs (Bundy)<anchor n="1" xml:id="d580-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1964-03-18">March 18, 1964</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <list type="references">
                    <head>RE</head>
                    <item>Your Meeting with Ambassador Naude
                    </item>
                </list>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>On February 12 US and British Ambassadors in Capetown presented parallel
                        aide-memoires urging South African Government postpone implementation of
                        Odendaal report apartheid policy in SWA
                        pending ICJ decision.<anchor n="2"
                            xml:id="d580-f2"/> They noted that we have urged other UN members to sit still awaiting ICJ decision and could not restrain them if
                        South Africa moved.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>In aide-memoire reply of March 3<anchor n="3" xml:id="d580-f3"/> South
                        African Government insisted its SW A administration is “in spirit of the
                        mandate”; that it cannot <pb facs="1023" n="975" xml:id="pg975"/> postpone
                        Odendaal implementation because of ICJ case;
                        that it can separate “overall design” of report from inoffensive “economic
                        welfare benefits”; that “separate development has been fundamental to
                        administration of SWA from the beginning and
                        is not new issue”; denies that implementation of Odendaal would justify
                        cease and desist order.</item>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item>US and British Ambassadors submitting second aide-memoire this week
                            (attached)<anchor n="4" xml:id="d580-f4"/> asserting—that South African
                        implementation would precipitate ICJ action;
                        that if Security Council called upon to support cease and desist order USG
                        would “respect authority of ICJ” and “could
                        not be expected to shield ICJ consequences
                        of its action”. Urges SAG to wait for final
                            ICJ decision.</item>
                    <label>4.</label>
                    <item>It might be very useful to draw Naude out on what it would take to move South Africa toward
                        some accommodation.</item>
                </list>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#bold">Bill Brubeck</hi>
                        <anchor n="5" xml:id="d580-f5"/>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
